// xrcefrag.nv: a sample longer than one message, joined from the FRAGMENT
// submessages the agent sends it in, and cut into the same pieces to be sent.
//
// `[]` THROUGHOUT and `@tier(embedded)` on all of it, with no allocation.
// The pieces are joined in a `Vec[u8; XRCE_REASSEMBLY_BYTES]` that the caller
// owns and passes by `var`, and the state is a `@value` of three small fields, so a
// device holds both beside its session and a host holds the same.
//
// WHY THERE ARE FRAGMENTS.  A message is no longer than the MTU the client
// named when it opened the session, and this package names 512.  The agent
// writes what DDS delivers as one DATA submessage, and a DATA longer than
// the room in one message is cut into pieces: the agent serialises the
// whole submessage, header included, and sends it as a run of messages on
// the reliable stream, each holding one FRAGMENT submessage whose payload
// is the next piece (Micro-XRCE-DDS-Agent, `ReliableOutputStream::
// push_submessage`).  The last piece has bit 1 of its flags set.  A
// best-effort stream has no fragments: a DATA that does not fit is not sent,
// so a node that wants samples longer than one message asks for them on
// the reliable stream.
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE DOES.  `push` takes one received message.  The reliable
// stream delivers messages in order (`xrcestream.delivers`), so the pieces
// arrive in order and a piece is appended to the buffer.  At the last
// piece the buffer holds one DATA submessage: its header, the request id
// and the object id, and the sample.  `push` checks that, moves the sample
// to the start of the buffer, and answers it as an `XrceSample` whose
// `start` is 0 and whose `length` is the buffer's length.
//
// A SAMPLE THAT DOES NOT FIT IS DROPPED, AND SAID SO.  The caller names the
// largest sample it will take, at most `XRCE_REASSEMBLY_MAX_SAMPLE`.  A
// piece that would take the buffer past it answers `FRAG_TOO_LONG` once,
// and the rest of that sample is dropped without a word until its last
// piece, so the next sample is joined from its own first piece.  A sample
// that came whole in one message is held to the same limit, so a node's
// answer to a sample does not depend on how the agent happened to send it.
//
// THE BUFFER HOLDS THE LAST SAMPLE UNTIL THE NEXT CALL.  A finished sample
// stays in the buffer for the caller to read, and `begin`, which every poll
// calls first, clears it.
//
// THE OTHER DIRECTION IS CUT, NOT JOINED.  A sample a node writes that does not
// fit one message goes out as the same run of messages on the reliable
// stream, each holding one FRAGMENT whose bytes are the next piece of the
// WRITE_DATA, header included, and the last with bit 1 of its flags set
// (`piece_begin` starts a piece and `xrcewire.finish_fragment` ends it; the
// session modules loop).  Each piece takes a sequence number and a slot of
// the history, so the HEARTBEAT and the ACKNACK cover it as they cover a
// whole message.  A piece holds up to `XRCE_PIECE_BYTES` and the history holds
// `xrcestream.XRCE_HISTORY` messages, so a write puts at most four pieces on
// the line before the agent acknowledges one: 2,016 bytes of WRITE_DATA, a
// sample of 2,008.  A longer sample is written in turns, with the caller's
// `at` saying how far it got.  The history is not grown, so a node that
// writes long samples pays no RAM for it; what it pays is one wait for an
// acknowledgement, a HEARTBEAT period, in the middle of a sample longer
// than 2,008 bytes.  The largest sample is `XRCE_LONG_MAX_SAMPLE`, 2,048,
// the size of the buffer a device holds it in.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT DO.  The agent can give up on messages it no longer
// holds, and a HEARTBEAT that says so moves the input side past them
// (`xrcestream.in_heartbeat`).  If that falls in the middle of a sample the
// pieces joined so far and the pieces of the next sample are joined into
// one, which is not a DATA, and `push` answers `FRAG_BAD` and starts over.
// That costs the two samples and is not mistaken for either.

use xrcewire

// The bytes of the buffer a caller passes: 2048.
//
// What the agent sends is the DATA submessage, so the buffer holds its four
// bytes of header, four bytes of request and object ids, and the sample.
pub const XRCE_REASSEMBLY_BYTES = 2048

// The largest sample a buffer holds: 2040, the buffer less the eight bytes
// in front of the sample.
pub const XRCE_REASSEMBLY_MAX_SAMPLE = 2040

// The most bytes of one piece: 504, the message's 512 less the message header
// (4) and the FRAGMENT's submessage header (4).
pub const XRCE_PIECE_BYTES = 504

// The largest sample a node writes in pieces: 2048, the size of the buffer a
// device holds it in. A longer one is refused with `ROS_RET_INVALID_ARGUMENT`.
pub const XRCE_LONG_MAX_SAMPLE = 2048

// The bytes in front of a sample in a DATA: the submessage header (4) and
// the request id and the object id (2 each).
const DATA_FRONT = 8

// The message held no FRAGMENT: the buffer and the state are as they were.
pub const FRAG_NONE = 0
// A piece was kept and more are to come, or a piece of a sample already
// dropped was dropped as well.
pub const FRAG_MORE = 1
// The last piece was kept: the sample is in the buffer, from its first byte.
pub const FRAG_DONE = 2
// The sample is longer than the caller allowed, and is dropped.
pub const FRAG_TOO_LONG = 3
// The pieces joined were not one DATA, and are dropped.
pub const FRAG_BAD = 4

// What the reassembly has decided about the sample in progress.
//
// A `@value` of a number and two flags.  The pieces themselves are in the caller's
// buffer, whose length is how much has been joined.
pub @value
struct FragState
    // The most bytes of a DATA the buffer is to hold, from 8 to
    // `XRCE_REASSEMBLY_BYTES`.
    limit: Int
    // Whether the rest of the sample in progress is being dropped, because
    // it did not fit.
    dropping: Bool
    // Whether the buffer holds a finished sample that `begin` is to clear.
    finished: Bool

// What `push` did with one message.
pub @value
struct FragEvent
    // The state after the message.
    assembly: FragState
    // One of `FRAG_*`.
    code: Int
    // The sample, when `code` is `FRAG_DONE`: `found`, the request id, the
    // data reader's object id, `start` 0 and `length` the sample's length in
    // the buffer.  Otherwise `found` is false.
    sample: xrcewire.XrceSample

// A reassembly that takes samples of at most `max_sample` bytes.
//
// A number above `XRCE_REASSEMBLY_MAX_SAMPLE` is taken as that, and a
// number below 0 as 0, so the limit is never more than the buffer holds.
//
// ```novo
// use xrcefrag
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${xrcefrag.reassembly(1000).limit}")   // 1008
//     println("${xrcefrag.reassembly(5000).limit}")   // 2048
// ```
pub @tier(embedded)
@no_alloc
fn reassembly(max_sample: Int) -> FragState []
    var limit = max_sample + DATA_FRONT
    if limit > XRCE_REASSEMBLY_BYTES
        limit = XRCE_REASSEMBLY_BYTES
    if limit < DATA_FRONT
        limit = DATA_FRONT
    FragState { limit: limit, dropping: false, finished: false }

// An event with this code and no sample.
pub @tier(embedded)
@no_alloc
fn event(state: FragState, code: Int) -> FragEvent []
    FragEvent { assembly: state, code: code,
                sample: xrcewire.XrceSample { found: false, request: 0, object: 0, start: 0,
                                              length: 0 } }

// The state at the start of a poll: a finished sample from the last poll is
// cleared out of the buffer, and the state forgets it.
//
// Call it before anything else in a poll, so that a caller reading the
// buffer after the poll sees one sample and not two.
pub @tier(embedded)
@no_alloc
fn begin(state: FragState, var a: Vec[u8; 2048]) -> FragState []
    if not state.finished
        return state
    a.clear()
    FragState { limit: state.limit, dropping: state.dropping, finished: false }

// Take a sample that came whole in one message, and hold it in the buffer
// as a joined one is.
//
// `sample` is what `xrcewire.read_sample` found in `m`.  It is copied to the
// start of the buffer, and the answer is `FRAG_DONE`, or `FRAG_TOO_LONG`
// when it is longer than `state.limit` allows, in which case the buffer stays
// empty.  This is what makes the buffer the one place a caller reads a
// sample from, whether it came in one message or in ten.
pub @tier(embedded)
@no_alloc
fn keep_sample(var m: Vec[u8; 512], sample: xrcewire.XrceSample, state: FragState,
               var a: Vec[u8; 2048]) -> FragEvent []
    a.clear()
    if sample.length + DATA_FRONT > state.limit
        return event(state, FRAG_TOO_LONG)
    for i in 0..sample.length
        let _ = a.push(m[sample.start + i])
    FragEvent { assembly: FragState { limit: state.limit, dropping: false, finished: true },
                code: FRAG_DONE,
                sample: xrcewire.XrceSample { found: true, request: sample.request,
                                              object: sample.object, start: 0,
                                              length: sample.length } }

// Move the sample of a joined DATA to the start of the buffer and read
// it, or answer `found = false` when the buffer is not one DATA.
//
// A DATA is its header (id 9, a flags byte whose bits 1 to 3 are the data
// format, a 16-bit length) and then that many bytes: the request id, the
// object id and the sample.  The format must be `XRCE_FORMAT_DATA`, the one
// `read_data` asks for, and the length must account for every byte joined.
@tier(embedded)
@no_alloc
fn take_data(var a: Vec[u8; 2048]) -> xrcewire.XrceSample []
    let none = xrcewire.XrceSample { found: false, request: 0, object: 0, start: 0, length: 0 }
    if a.len() < DATA_FRONT
        return none
    let length = a[2] as Int + a[3] as Int * 256
    if (a[0] as Int) != xrcewire.XRCE_SUB_DATA or length != a.len() - 4
        return none
    if (a[1] as Int & xrcewire.XRCE_FORMAT_MASK) != xrcewire.XRCE_FORMAT_DATA
        return none
    let request = a[4] as Int * 256 + a[5] as Int
    let object = a[6] as Int * 256 + a[7] as Int
    let n = a.len() - DATA_FRONT
    for i in 0..n
        a[i] = a[i + DATA_FRONT]
    for _k in 0..DATA_FRONT
        let _ = a.pop()
    xrcewire.XrceSample { found: true, request: request, object: object, start: 0, length: n }

// Take one received message, and join its piece if it holds one.
//
// `m` is the message, `state` the state and `a` the buffer, empty before the
// first piece of a sample.  Call it for a message the reliable stream
// delivered in order (`xrcestream.delivers`); a message that holds no
// FRAGMENT answers `FRAG_NONE` and changes nothing.  Otherwise:
//
//   - `FRAG_MORE`: the piece is kept, and more are to come.
//   - `FRAG_DONE`: the last piece is kept and `sample` says where the sample
//     is: `a[0]` to `a[sample.length - 1]`.  The buffer is left as it is for
//     the caller to read, and `begin` clears it at the next poll.
//   - `FRAG_TOO_LONG`: the sample is longer than `state.limit` allows, the
//     buffer is cleared, and the rest of this sample is dropped.
//   - `FRAG_BAD`: the pieces were not one DATA, and the buffer is cleared.
//
// ```novo
// use xrcefrag
// use xrcewire
//
// fn main() [io]
//     var m: Vec[u8; 512] = Vec.new()
//     var a: Vec[u8; 2048] = Vec.new()
//     let ev = xrcefrag.push(var m, xrcefrag.reassembly(100), var a)
//     println("${ev.code}")   // 0, because the message holds no fragment
// ```
pub @tier(embedded)
@no_alloc
fn push(var m: Vec[u8; 512], state: FragState, var a: Vec[u8; 2048]) -> FragEvent []
    let frag = xrcewire.read_fragment(var m)
    if not frag.found
        return event(state, FRAG_NONE)
    if state.dropping
        // The rest of a sample that did not fit, up to its last piece.
        return event(FragState { limit: state.limit, dropping: not frag.last, finished: false },
                     FRAG_MORE)
    if a.len() + frag.length > state.limit
        a.clear()
        return event(FragState { limit: state.limit, dropping: not frag.last, finished: false },
                     FRAG_TOO_LONG)
    for i in 0..frag.length
        let _ = a.push(m[frag.start + i])
    if not frag.last
        return event(state, FRAG_MORE)
    let sample = take_data(var a)
    if not sample.found
        a.clear()
        return event(state, FRAG_BAD)
    FragEvent { assembly: FragState { limit: state.limit, dropping: false, finished: true },
                code: FRAG_DONE, sample: sample }

// ── Writing in pieces ────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Start the message of the next piece of a sample, and answer whether it
// fitted.
//
// `seq` is the sequence number the message takes and `at` how many bytes of
// the sample are already written; `length` is the sample's length, `request`
// the id of the write and `object` the writer's, or for a reply the replier's.
// A piece at `at` 0 is the first, and begins with the WRITE_DATA's front, for a
// reply with `reply` true and the request's `identity` after the ids (see
// `xrcewire.put_write_front`); any other piece is sample bytes alone. The
// caller appends `xrcefrag.piece_room` bytes at most, and ends the message with
// `xrcewire.finish_fragment`, naming the piece last when it wrote the rest.
pub @tier(embedded)
@no_alloc
fn piece_begin(var m: Vec[u8; 512], seq: Int, at: Int, length: Int, request: Int, object: Int,
               reply: Bool, identity: xrcewire.XrceIdentity) -> Bool []
    var ok = xrcewire.begin_fragment(var m, seq)
    if at > 0
        return ok
    if reply
        return ok and xrcewire.put_reply_front(var m, length, request, object, identity)
    ok and xrcewire.put_write_front(var m, length, request, object)

// How many more bytes a piece message takes.
pub @tier(embedded)
@no_alloc
fn piece_room(var m: Vec[u8; 512]) -> Int []
    512 - m.len()

// Copy the first `n` bytes of the joined sample into `m`, replacing what it
// held, or all of them when there are fewer. Answers how many were copied.
//
// A request's identity and a reply's request id are read out of the first
// bytes of a sample by `xrcewire.request_of` and `xrcewire.response_of`, which
// read a message, so a long sample's first bytes are put in one to be read.
pub @tier(embedded)
@no_alloc
fn copy_head(var a: Vec[u8; 2048], var m: Vec[u8; 512], n: Int) -> Int []
    m.clear()
    for i in 0..n
        if i < a.len()
            let _ = m.push(a[i])
    m.len()
